  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Elastic Saddle (Helvella elastica) is a small, delicate fungus in the Helvellaceae family, characterized by its saddle-shaped or brain-like fruiting body. This ascomycete is commonly found in wooded areas throughout New England during spring and early summer.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Saddle-shaped cap",
      "description": "The distinctive cap is deeply lobed and resembles a saddle or brain with irregular, wavy edges",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Hollow stem",
      "description": "The stem is hollow, thin, and typically white to pale, often matching or slightly lighter than the cap color",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Color variability",
      "description": "The fruiting body ranges from pale tan to gray or brownish, with a somewhat fragile and elastic texture",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Helvella elastica grows in moist, well-drained soils in mixed and hardwood forests, often appearing near decaying wood and rich leaf litter.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The genus name 'Helvella' comes from the Latin word for 'saddle,' perfectly describing the fungus's most distinctive feature that gives it both its common and scientific names.",
